Hi All,

I'm also having the same problem. I haven't yet tried introducing a random offset 'jitter', and am wondering if this is what would you would suggest given my situation?

I have upgraded to the latest test version of INLA.

I'm running multiple spde models with slightly different mesh constructs on each occasion. My data consists of an original transect that has quadrats along it with binomial outcomes. I'm then simulating offset transcets with a random angle of offset and displacement distance. When I run small test batches (e.g. 10 -20) then everything runs fine. However, when I run a larger number of simulations (>100) I usually get the fmesher64 hang up. I haven't yet been able to reproduce this on smaller or single runs, so I'm not sure what might be causing this. Could it be when I get a random offset that is very close to the original, and so my mesh vertices are almost on top of each other? My original transect consists of a series of longer 'legs' in an east-west direction linked by short legs in a north-south direction. I wouldn't describe it as a particularly regular design as is the case for the OP.

Currently I'm estimating this will take weeks to run without encountering this error, so I really would like to find a solution. Any advice?

Thanks in advance.

Nick

